Children's cortisol patterns were divided into three groups: hypercortisol (68%), characterized by a persistent elevation in cortisol; insufficient cortisol (9%), characterized by a persistent decrease in cortisol; and hypercortisol-to-insufficient cortisol (23%) where cortisol levels start high before declining to normal levels. Children in the hypercortisol-to-insufficiency group experienced fewer mental health issues (both internal and external symptoms) compared to those in the hypercortisol-free group. Children in the hypocortisol group did not show any significant difference in psychological symptoms compared to healthy children in the hypercortisol-high group.
